1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,417
This is the Restatement Risk Agent, built for the Vultr hackathon, Challenge One, Blast Radius Zero.

2
00:00:07,867 --> 00:00:15,227
When a public company files an eight-K under Item four point oh two, it is telling investors not to rely on its past financial statements.

3
00:00:15,677 --> 00:00:18,057
What changed, and what has the SEC said?

4
00:00:18,507 --> 00:00:24,185
The SEC does not audit companies. Its follow-up comes as comment letters and, less often, enforcement.

5
00:00:25,635 --> 00:00:27,098
Here is how it runs on Vultr.

6
00:00:27,548 --> 00:00:34,676
A web app on a Vultr VM fetches public SEC data and asks Vultr Serverless Inference to write Python analysis code.

7
00:00:35,126 --> 00:00:44,310
A separate runner, with the Docker socket but no keys, runs it in a throwaway container: no network, read-only, hard limits on memory, CPU and time.

8
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:51,356
The home page takes a ticker, and lists recent companies that filed Item four point oh two notices.

9
00:00:52,806 --> 00:00:57,496
Here is GoPro, which filed a non-reliance notice in October twenty twenty six.

10
00:00:57,946 --> 00:01:02,590
The model writes the explanation, from numbers computed by deterministic reference code.

11
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:09,984
This table shows each line item where the originally filed number differs from the latest, with links to both filings.

12
00:01:10,434 --> 00:01:14,057
Long-term debt, for example, moved by about seventeen percent.

13
00:01:15,507 --> 00:01:22,229
Below: the amended filings, SEC comment-letter correspondence, and any enforcement release naming the company.

14
00:01:23,679 --> 00:01:25,548
This is why the reference code exists.

15
00:01:25,998 --> 00:01:32,941
The model's sandboxed script computed a Dechow F-score of one point three seven, which would cross the screen's flag line.

16
00:01:33,391 --> 00:01:38,720
The reference code gets zero point nine seven. The page flags the mismatch and uses the reference numbers.

17
00:01:40,170 --> 00:01:47,821
The ask box takes a free-form question. Here: pull PetMed Express's latest amended annual report and summarize what was restated.

18
00:01:48,271 --> 00:01:51,034
Nothing in the prompt tells the model to use the internet.

19
00:01:52,484 --> 00:01:56,373
The model wrote code that tried to download the filing from sec dot gov anyway.

20
00:01:56,823 --> 00:02:03,394
The sandbox has no network, so the lookup failed. The banner shows it, and the answer says plainly that the document was never read.

21
00:02:04,844 --> 00:02:12,867
An audit hook inside the sandbox records each network attempt, so the event shows even when the code swallows the error. Every attempt is logged.

22
00:02:14,317 --> 00:02:18,218
The backtest page is a record of what happened, not an accuracy claim.

23
00:02:18,668 --> 00:02:28,978
Of one thousand thirty-one companies that filed Item four point oh two notices from twenty twenty one to twenty twenty three, twenty later appear by name in an SEC enforcement release.

24
00:02:30,428 --> 00:02:36,859
Many rows show insufficient data, mostly blank-check companies and tagging gaps. We show that instead of guessing.

25
00:02:38,309 --> 00:02:44,916
The scores are screens, not predictions, and approximate published academic models, not the SEC's internal tools.

26
00:02:45,366 --> 00:02:51,321
The model makes mistakes. That is why the reference code checks its numbers, and why its code runs where it can do no harm.

27
00:02:52,771 --> 00:02:58,542
The Restatement Risk Agent. Built on Vultr Cloud Compute and Vultr Serverless Inference. Thanks for watching.

